  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-950">Mountaineering expeditions in Pakistan</h2>
  <p>We run expeditions from Skardu to Pakistan's 8,000-metre peaks and to the 6,000- and 7,000-metre peaks of the Karakoram. We handle the permit, the liaison officer, transport, hotels, porters to base camp and a full base camp, and we add high-altitude support where the climb needs it.</p>
  <p><strong>8,000-metre peaks.</strong> <a href="/expeditions/k2-expedition/">K2</a> (8,611 m), <a href="/expeditions/nanga-parbat-expedition/">Nanga Parbat</a> (8,126 m), <a href="/expeditions/gasherbrum-i-expedition/">Gasherbrum I</a> (8,080 m), <a href="/expeditions/broad-peak-expedition/">Broad Peak</a> (8,051 m) and <a href="/expeditions/gasherbrum-ii-expedition/">Gasherbrum II</a> (8,035 m). Four of them share the Baltoro approach, so two peaks can be climbed from one trip.</p>
  <p><strong>7,000 metres and below.</strong> <a href="/expeditions/spantik-peak-expedition/">Spantik</a> (7,027 m) is a good first 7,000-metre peak. <a href="/expeditions/laila-peak-expedition/">Laila Peak</a> above Hushe and the <a href="/expeditions/trango-expedition/">Trango Towers</a> are for technical climbers.</p>
  <p><strong>Permits and royalty.</strong> K2 carries a royalty of USD 3,500, the other 8,000-metre peaks USD 2,500 and 7,000-metre peaks USD 2,000. Peaks below 7,000 metres are climbed on a trekking permit. See our guide to <a href="/blog/pakistan-mountaineering-royalty-fees/">Pakistan royalty fees</a>.</p>
